WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.040
Narrator: A lot of
people aren't comfortable

00:00:02.040 --> 00:00:04.010
swimming in open water.

00:00:04.010 --> 00:00:06.200
I mean, you never know what lives

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:08.280
in the water beneath your feet.

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:12.053
The ocean holds many
bizarre deep sea monsters.

00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:16.140
As you dive 140 meters underwater,

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:19.200
you might see a megamouth shark.

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:22.060
Sure, they look scary, but those 50 rows

00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:24.663
of teeth are for filtering krill.

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:28.400
The Japanese spider crab is happy

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:31.360
to welcome you 300 meters down.

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:33.310
These massive crustaceans are thought

00:00:33.310 --> 00:00:37.433
to live to 100 years old
and are a Japanese delicacy.

00:00:38.750 --> 00:00:41.910
The frilled shark looks closer to an eel.

00:00:41.910 --> 00:00:45.750
Its needle-like teeth hook
squid one half its size

00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:48.233
and its jaws can gulp them down.

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:52.260
Even deeper is the Pacific blackdragon.

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:57.260
It uses its chin barbel as a
lure to attract small fish.

00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:02.130
Another 150 meters down,
we meet the vampire squid.

00:01:02.130 --> 00:01:05.250
It has bioluminescent
organs called photophores

00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:07.340
that produce flashes of light.

00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:09.010
But it doesn't drink blood.

00:01:09.010 --> 00:01:12.760
It prefers free-floating
debris from the surface.

00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:16.420
The deeper you go the
more alien things look.

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:18.290
Goblin sharks are believed

00:01:18.290 --> 00:01:22.160
to be unchanged for 25 million years,

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:24.750
basically making them living fossils.

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:28.310
They can launch their
jaws forward to grab prey.

00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:30.750
Look, here comes the blobfish!

00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:32.550
This thrilling deep-sea fish

00:01:32.550 --> 00:01:35.070
was voted the world's ugliest animal

00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:38.440
by the Ugly Animal Preservation Society.

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:40.070
But its jelly-like skin looks

00:01:40.070 --> 00:01:43.710
much more natural at 900 meters.

00:01:43.710 --> 00:01:46.820
Down here, we enter the midnight zone

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:49.990
where no natural light can reach.

00:01:49.990 --> 00:01:54.460
You might also pass the
Tiburonia granrojo, or big red,

00:01:54.460 --> 00:01:57.563
one of the largest jellyfish in the world.

00:01:58.610 --> 00:02:01.170
The fangtooth has teeth to spare,

00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:03.220
the largest of any fish.

00:02:03.220 --> 00:02:05.933
It can't even fully close its mouth.

00:02:06.930 --> 00:02:10.920
The barreleye looks upward
through its translucent head.

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:13.320
It recognizes the silhouette of its prey

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:15.203
in even the most dim light.

00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:19.690
But it should watch out
below for the ghost shark.

00:02:19.690 --> 00:02:22.320
Its body is covered with sensory organs

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:25.790
that detect motion in
the surrounding water.

00:02:25.790 --> 00:02:28.300
Deeper down are giant isopods,

00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:30.820
supersized crustaceans.

00:02:30.820 --> 00:02:34.113
These guys are closely
related to common pill bugs.

00:02:35.020 --> 00:02:37.230
Down at 2200 meters is one

00:02:37.230 --> 00:02:39.860
of the biggest residents of the deep.

00:02:39.860 --> 00:02:42.910
At 14 meters long, the colossal squid

00:02:42.910 --> 00:02:45.410
is the largest known invertebrate.

00:02:45.410 --> 00:02:49.000
Its arms have sharp hooks,
which it uses to catch prey

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:51.730
and fight sperm whales.

00:02:51.730 --> 00:02:54.900
As we go even deeper,
we enter the hadal zone,

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:56.940
where life is less common.

00:02:56.940 --> 00:03:01.220
The sea devil is the
quintessential deep-sea anglerfish.

00:03:01.220 --> 00:03:03.930
Its bioluminescent lure attracts prey

00:03:03.930 --> 00:03:06.900
close to its massive jaws.

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:09.460
But you can still find
some extreme life forms

00:03:09.460 --> 00:03:12.960
as deep as 7,000 meters under the surface.

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:15.510
Like our friend, the sea spider.

00:03:15.510 --> 00:03:20.000
It sucks up worms from the
ocean floor with its proboscis.

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:22.270
There are potentially thousands more

00:03:22.270 --> 00:03:25.920
undiscovered creatures
swimming around underneath us.

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:28.683
Who knows what else might
be living down there.

